WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.750
I tested an agent that a student

2
00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:04.710
wrote, and it never stopped!

3
00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:09.470
Call, result, call, result.

4
00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:13.830
No answer, ever, until the battery died.

5
00:00:15.110 --> 00:00:17.390
Today: the agent loop in Python,

6
00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:19.290
and how to make it stop.

7
00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:23.510
An agent is a loop around a model.

8
00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:26.320
Ask the model for its next output.

9
00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:28.380
If it’s an answer, you’re done.

10
00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:31.730
If it’s a call, run the tool, add both

11
00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:33.570
to the history, and ask again.

12
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.320
But the Python sandbox has no

13
00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:38.400
language model inside.

14
00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:41.990
So in your code, a scripted model

15
00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:44.950
stands in: a function that returns what

16
00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:46.750
a model would write, from a list.

17
00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:50.370
It’s not intelligent, and we say so.

18
00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:52.650
It lets you test your loop.

19
00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:56.260
The history is everything so far:

20
00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.260
the request, then each call and

21
00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:00.600
its result, in order.

22
00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:03.720
A real model reads it all on every

23
00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:05.880
turn: that’s its only memory.

24
00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:09.260
Your loop builds it, one turn at a time.

25
00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:11.140
Here’s the loop.

26
00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:13.880
For each step, ask the model.

27
00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:16.280
No curly bracket means an

28
00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:17.900
answer, so return it.

29
00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:20.550
Otherwise, run the tool, and

30
00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:21.750
add both to the history.

31
00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:26.610
Why for step in range, and not while true?

32
00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:29.590
Because a model can get stuck, calling

33
00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:31.990
tool after tool without answering.

34
00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:35.260
A step limit is a promise: after

35
00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:37.100
four steps, the loop stops,

36
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:38.500
whatever the model writes.

37
00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:40.330
Quick check!

38
00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:44.260
The script only ever returns calls, never

39
00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:47.060
an answer, and the limit is four.

40
00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.380
What does the agent return?

41
00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:53.080
Stopped, after four steps.

42
00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:55.680
That’s the step limit doing its job.

43
00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.400
In a real app, you’d also tell the

44
00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.100
person: I couldn’t finish this.

45
00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:02.400
Never hide a stop.

46
00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:05.800
Some goals need two tools.

47
00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:09.800
The trip from Kafi to Dala calls route,

48
00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:12.860
then weather, using the first result.

49
00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.120
But a step can fail.

50
00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:17.820
Asked for six B’s first lesson on

51
00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:20.680
Saturday, Darsi Tiny got no school,

52
00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:22.540
and still set a reminder.

53
00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:26.390
In the lab, guess which tools a

54
00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:28.730
goal needs, then watch Darsi

55
00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:30.450
Tiny plan and call them.

56
00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:33.140
Run the Saturday goal, and

57
00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:34.480
see the failed step.

58
00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:36.680
Your loop could stop there,

59
00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:38.160
with a check in code.

60
00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:40.190
Your turn.

61
00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:43.210
Your loop passes every test

62
00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:44.230
with the scripted model.

63
00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:45.680
What have you shown?

64
00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:48.530
That your loop works, for

65
00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:49.770
the outputs you tested.

66
00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:53.530
Remember: the loop asks the model, runs

67
00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:55.470
its calls and builds the history.

68
00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:58.460
A step limit stops a stuck model.

69
00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:00.880
And a scripted model tests

70
00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:02.660
your code, not the model.

71
00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:05.520
Try it yourself: play the

72
00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:06.600
loop with two friends.

73
00:03:07.330 --> 00:03:09.250
One is the model, reading

74
00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:10.090
from a script card.

75
00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:11.930
One is the tool.

76
00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:14.760
You are the loop: count the

77
00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.600
steps, and stop at four.

78
00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:19.380
Then try a script that never answers.
